    "case_id": "320",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 50s with a 10-year history of Crohn disease had intermittent bowel obstructions leading to total proctocolectomy and ileostomy. Five weeks after his surgery, he developed a painful, erosive lesion around his stoma on the anterior abdominal wall. No additional cutaneous or mucosal lesions were present.\n\nPainful, erosive lesion present around the stoma on the anterior abdominal wall\n\n- Microbiological cultures: Repeated cultures were negative- Histopathology:   - Revealed epidermal erosion with suprabasal epidermal splitting with acantholysis  - Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin showed intercellular deposition of IgG and C3 throughout epidermis- Laboratory tests:  - Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay showed high level of IgG autoantibodies:    - Against desmoglein 1: 40 IU/mL (normal value: 20 U/mL)    - Against desmoglein 3: 90 IU/mL (normal value: 20 U/mL)- Images:  Figure 1A Title: Erosive lesion around stoma  Description: A painful, erosive lesion around the stoma on the anterior abdominal wall  Figure 1B Title: Histopathology  Description: Hematoxylin-eosin stain showing suprabasal epidermal splitting with acantholysis (original magnification ×100)",
